Summary

Nuxt compared routeRules keys against the raw request path, without decoding percent-encoding, while the router decodes parameters when it resolves a route. A request whose path is percent-encoded can therefore resolve to a page while failing to match the route rule meant to protect it, so the rule's appMiddleware never runs, including during server rendering.

Impact

For a page served through a dynamic or catch-all route, an attacker can percent-encode a character in the path to skip the rule:

export default defineNuxtConfig({
  routeRules: {
    '/users/admin': { appMiddleware: 'auth' },
  },
})

Requesting /users/%61dmin still renders app/pages/users/[id].vue with id decoded to admin, but the rule keyed /users/admin does not match, so auth does not run and the server renders and returns the gated page to an unauthenticated visitor. No privileges and no user interaction are required, and only ASCII characters are involved.

Purely static ASCII routes are not affected: stat

1. Summary
The API endpoint POST /api/v1/@apostrophecms/import-export/clean-export in @apostrophecms/import-export lacks authentication and authorization checks. Any client—including unauthenticated anonymous users who have obtained the standard CSRF cookie from visiting the site—can directly invoke this route and trigger the underlying self.cleanExport(req) method.

2. Affected Files & Endpoints
* Endpoint: POST /api/v1/@apostrophecms/import-export/clean-export
* Route Definition: packages/import-export/lib/apiRoutes.js (lines 32–34)
* Method Implementation: packages/import-export/lib/methods/import.js (lines 1271–1324)

3. Root Cause Analysis
In packages/import-export/lib/apiRoutes.js, the get.related handler explicitly validates that the caller is logged in:
get: {
  async related(req) {
    if (!req.user) {
      throw self.apos.error('forbidden');
    }
    // ...
  }
}
In contrast, post.cleanExport (as well as post.overrideDuplicates) lacks any verification of req.user:
post: {

The GHSA-9rhm fix added this safe setting in
AndroidA2AHttpClient.createConnection():

    connection.setInstanceFollowRedirects(false);

The synchronous POST builder later overwrites it:

    private boolean followRedirects = true;

    public A2AHttpResponse post() throws IOException {
        HttpURLConnection connection = createConnection("POST", false);
        connection.setInstanceFollowRedirects(followRedirects);
        // ...
    }

The second assignment wins. JSONRPCTransport and RestTransport both
add interceptor-produced headers and call synchronous post() without
setting followRedirects(false).

On Android's repackaged OkHttp implementation, a cross-authority
redirect strips Authorization but retains nonstandard request headers.
AuthInterceptor permits four nonstandard authentication header names:

    X-API-Key
    API-Key
    X-Auth-Token
    X-Authentication

I confirmed that the exact v1.3.1.Final Maven artifact forwards
complete synthetic values under all four names

